Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Young Tableaux", "Title",ExpressionUUID->"6ee1098c-6972-4394-b959-14b686b1b164"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Young tableaux are combinatorial pictures of numbered boxes, and for a \
physicist they are the one bookkeeping device that links three things you \
already use: the symmetry types of tensors with ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "e78e7c9e-0785-4fa1-bab0-d4ca6675c457"],
 " indices, the irreducible blocks of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "8dac6a38-eb82-4b41-a80a-6c4df60867a3"],
 "-qubit Hilbert space under collective rotations, and the explicit \
projectors that pull each symmetry sector out of a state. This note builds \
the picture from scratch using the paclet's ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  StyleBox["Wolfram`TensorNetworks`Symmetry", "InlineCode"]], "InlineCode",
  ExpressionUUID->"a50741d2-da37-43bc-bb32-d69a68d8baec"],
 " `` subcontext, with quantum-state examples (Bell, singlet, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"|", 
    StyleBox["W", "TI"], "\[RightAngleBracket]"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"04f5d316-3577-41c9-83a8-38240ab5ca72"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"|", "001"}], "\[RightAngleBracket]"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"2de50cf2-eda9-465f-b373-55ed955326b0"],
 ") constructed through the TN paclet."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"93404e7c-1e82-4b3f-a6c4-e41bf3b8c19a"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Why a Young diagram?", "Section",ExpressionUUID->"44c39174-85f6-43cb-8840-c4c9f4d8b83c"],

Cell["\<\
A Young diagram is a picture of an integer partition: boxes arranged in \
left-justified rows of weakly decreasing length. Why a quantum researcher \
should care, in one picture.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"25bb0516-1a26-470f-bb9c-d245171f23b3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Take ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "45095a3c-59a2-4fb6-aa32-8a526f53bdb6"],
 " qubits and consider the operations that act on all of them at once: a \
global SU(2) rotation ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["U", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"\[CircleTimes]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]}]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"74dab5fa-a074-413e-9123-309effb1d938"],
 " (the same rotation on each qubit), and a permutation that shuffles the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7c33edb3-072d-4b0e-aff7-7bd706bad39a"],
 " qubits. These two families of operations commute with each other, and \
together they split the Hilbert space ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["(", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "2d8ea616-c75c-44d0-89a8-71fabe749c50"],
 " into independent blocks that neither family can mix. The block labels are \
the Young diagrams of size ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "64903894-1306-4f92-8029-529ba4693277"],
 ". Each diagram pins down one specific total spin ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["J", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "ebdcb646-b001-4aae-b156-d87d3ffe39bf"],
 ", and within that spin sector there may be several independent copies (one \
per standard filling of the diagram)."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"76174bbd-ac43-43f3-aca1-5fd5dbf1c437"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Anything in your physics problem that has this collective symmetry (a \
Hamiltonian invariant under global SU(2), noise that hits all qubits \
identically, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "a36419bd-3470-44b5-8119-fd0249180610"],
 " identical copies of one quantum state) ",
 StyleBox["must", "TI"],
 " respect the decomposition. So before solving anything, you can replace \
\"diagonalize a ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    SuperscriptBox["2", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]], "\[Cross]", 
    SuperscriptBox["2", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"f67db4bc-27f7-4eb1-a8d3-84485d4b01ea"],
 " matrix\" with \"solve one much smaller problem inside each Young-diagram \
block\". That is the only reason for the rest of this note."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"f6ca9f9b-b554-4964-93de-4cde6d7d0671"],

Cell["\<\
Four concrete payoffs land in later sections, each one phrased here in \
language you already use.\
\>", "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8c25c1fb-46b3-426c-94a9-f686cc04318c"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Decoherence-free subspaces and noiseless subsystems.", "TI"],
 " If the noise on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "35ef6a5b-5c8b-4d55-b37b-5a8b08f61586"],
 " qubits is collective (the same single-qubit unitary ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["V", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "067836ba-11ce-419f-8b09-28c6661c0cce"],
 " on every qubit as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   SuperscriptBox[
    StyleBox["V", "TI"], 
    RowBox[{"\[CircleTimes]", 
     StyleBox["n", "TI"]}]], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",
  ExpressionUUID->"02a8b5b5-7dad-4548-86dd-a73052fc102c"],
 "), there are subspaces of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["(", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "7c06da20-11e9-458c-bd61-8d146ac58839"],
 " that carry collective-noise-protected logical information: the \
multiplicity space of each Young-diagram block is left untouched by the noise \
(a noiseless subsystem), and the spin-0 (singlet) blocks are even fixed \
pointwise (genuine decoherence-free subspaces). A logical qubit encoded in \
such a sector is automatically protected against collective dephasing, with \
no syndrome extraction. Young diagrams enumerate exactly these protected \
sectors."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"ac50adb8-bb9d-4c40-abca-3d559d2f29ec"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Spectrum estimation from independent and identically distributed \
samples.", "TI"],
 " Given ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c27b2bf4-7c0d-4a6e-8925-b6eb642318e9"],
 " identical copies of an unknown single-qubit state ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["\[Rho]", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "5713ec5b-e398-4885-8ef6-53597b740ab8"],
 ", measuring in a particular basis returns one discrete outcome (a Young \
diagram) whose distribution concentrates on ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["spec",
     FontSlant->"Plain"], "(", "\[Rho]", ")"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"90e3e7d7-e7a0-4a3e-8728-fdd7271b5c99"],
 " as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["n", "TI"], "\[Rule]", "\[Infinity]"}], TraditionalForm]], 
  "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->"25b77054-d102-4338-a539-b5efeae18eb2"],
 ". The Young diagram is the ",
 StyleBox["universal sufficient statistic", "TI"],
 " for spectrum estimation from independent and identically distributed \
samples, and the outcome probability for any given state is a single \
projection norm."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"11daa86d-2cd9-4a3d-8752-aecf92a32ea3"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Symmetric tensor networks.", "TI"],
 " An MPS / PEPS / MERA ansatz that preserves SU(2) (or any compact symmetry) \
has bond Hilbert spaces that automatically split into Young-diagram blocks. \
Contractions become block-diagonal, and the ansatz produces states with the \
correct total quantum-number sector by construction. The per-block sizes are \
computable ",
 StyleBox["before", "TI"],
 " you build any tensor."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"4b6f7b64-a19f-45f1-9364-32c5c8822b09"],

Cell[TextData[{
 StyleBox["Total angular momentum.", "TI"],
 " For ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "3095c5b3-4eb6-40fa-aed2-2f960db17138"],
 " qubits, the Hilbert space ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox["(", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "6399a2ba-8f29-4dbc-8cab-51c58798c536"],
 " decomposes into total-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["J", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "c967c206-dcff-4ffe-9035-55bfad2b4fd0"],
 " multiplets, the rank-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "588a75e3-ca6b-4182-9e7c-851329a498e1"],
 " analog of two-spin Clebsch-Gordan addition. Which spin-",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["J", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d7b98d53-c834-4bdd-b72c-51678e545284"],
 " appears, how many times, and with what block size, all read off Young \
diagrams."
}], "ItemNumbered",ExpressionUUID->"4438cde2-7084-482a-b389-979bafdcc0f4"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "These four are not separate tricks. They are ",
 StyleBox["one", "TI"],
 " construction (replace an ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["n", "TI"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "db1d7483-4ea3-427d-9ed2-d612787b2392"],
 "-qubit problem with one independent problem per Young-diagram block) read \
with four different physical questions in mind. The rest of this note builds \
the machinery in the order each payoff needs it: partitions and diagrams (the \
language), hook-length and hook-content formulas (the block sizes), \
projection operators built from a single tableau (how to land a state in one \
block), and worked quantum-state examples that realize each of the four \
payoffs above."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"dfc74819-f238-495b-a963-f3ed1ffce60e"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Load the paclet and pull the Symmetry subcontext into scope; both ",
 Cell[BoxData[
 "Needs"], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "9562d479-686b-4712-a4f8-4edaf401f719"],
 " are assumed in every subsequent cell."
}], "Text",ExpressionUUID->"8b94b392-6591-49eb-af83-77b0ec6861cc"],

Cell[BoxData[{
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
  "PacletDirectoryLoad", 
   "[", "\"\</Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/TensorNetworks\>\"", "]"}], 
  ";"}], "\n", 
 RowBox[{
